### Mandatory Coding Rules

These rules supplement the project Software Code Standards (SCS) document. Violations are findings.

```
1.  All functions shall have a file-level traceability header:
    @req, @design, @dal/@al, @status

2.  All functions at module boundaries shall validate all input
    parameters before processing (defensive input validation pattern).

3.  All functions shall return a status/error code. Void returns
    are permitted only for internal helper functions with no
    failure modes.

4.  All switch statements shall include a default case, even if
    all enum values are handled. The default case shall log an
    error and transition to a safe state.

5.  Dynamic memory allocation (malloc, calloc, realloc, free)
    is {{PROHIBITED / PERMITTED WITH CONSTRAINTS — specify}}.

6.  Recursion is {{PROHIBITED / PERMITTED only with provable
    termination and bounded stack depth — specify bound}}.

7.  Floating-point equality comparisons (==, !=) are prohibited.
    Use tolerance-based comparison functions.

8.  All magic numbers shall be defined as named constants with
    units documented in the name or comment.

9.  Global variables are {{PROHIBITED / PERMITTED WITH CONSTRAINTS
    — specify access control pattern}}.

10. Compiler warnings shall be treated as errors. The project
    compiles with {{-Wall -Werror / equivalent flags}}.

11. All #include dependencies shall be explicit. No reliance on
    transitive includes.

12. Deactivated code (code not intended to execute in this
    configuration) shall be identified with @deactivated annotation
    and verified to the same DAL/AL or safely isolated.

13. Dead code (code that can never execute) shall be removed.
    If removal is infeasible, it shall be documented with
    justification in the SAS.
```

