### Test Case Structure

Every test case must contain:

```markdown
## {{TST-ID}}: {{Title}}

@test     {{TST-ID}}
@req      {{Requirement(s) under test}}
@type     {{Normal / Boundary / Robustness / Error / Integration}}
@dal      {{Level}}
@status   DRAFT
@env      {{Host / Target / Simulator — specify}}

**Objective**: {{What is being verified and why}}

**Pre-conditions**: {{System/module state required before test}}

| Step | Input | Expected Output | Tolerance | Pass/Fail |
|------|-------|-----------------|-----------|-----------|
| 1    |       |                 |           |           |

**Pass/Fail Criteria**: {{Objective, unambiguous criteria}}

**Post-conditions**: {{Expected system state after test}}

**Notes**: {{Special instructions, known limitations, environment requirements}}
```

### Coverage Gap Resolution

When structural coverage analysis reveals gaps, resolve using **only** these methods (in order of preference):

1. **Additional requirements-based test cases** — Identify which requirement facet the uncovered code implements, write a test that exercises it from the requirements perspective.
2. **Dead code removal** — If the code can never execute and serves no purpose, remove it. Document the removal in a PR.
3. **Deactivated code analysis** — If the code is intentionally present but not active in this configuration, document why and verify it is safely isolated.
4. **Analysis justification** — If coverage cannot be achieved through testing (e.g., defensive code for hardware faults), provide written analysis justifying the gap. Include in SAS.

🔴 **Never write tests solely to achieve coverage targets.** Tests must always derive from requirements. Writing code-directed tests to close coverage gaps is a process violation.

